WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:39.773
- Hi everyone.

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:42.370
Welcome to our Homeroom livestream.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.810
Very excited about the
conversation we're about to have.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:47.010
But I will start with my
standard announcements

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:51.560
reminding everyone that we,
Khan Academy, we're a 501(c)(3).

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:52.530
We're not for profit.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.970
We can only exist with donations
from folks like yourself.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.100
So, if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.220
please think about making a donation.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.970
I'll also wanna give a special shout out

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:04.560
to several corporations and organizations

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.290
that really stepped up over
the last several months

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.069
when they realized that Khan Academy

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:12.380
was already running at
a deficit pre-COVID,

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.490
and our deficit only accelerated

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:18.730
as we had almost two to three
times the number of users

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.330
and we tried to accelerate
a whole series of programs

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.560
to support people through school closures.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.130
So, special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.090
AT&amp;T, Fastly, and Novartis.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.820
Although, even with their support,

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:31.700
their very generous support,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.480
we continue to run at a deficit.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.313
So, once again,

00:01:34.313 --> 00:01:37.100
if you represent a
corporation, or even yourself,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.730
every donation makes a difference.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:43.700
So, with that, I'm very excited
to introduce our next guest,

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.140
Dr. David Sinclair, professor
at Harvard University.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.193
David, thanks for joining.

00:01:50.193 --> 00:01:52.043
- Thanks, Sal. Good to see you again.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:54.453
- Likewise.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.470
Folks don't...

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.674
I mean, I have talked about in the past

00:01:58.674 --> 00:02:00.550
I have some fringe eating habits,

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:01.980
and so I'm definitely very in

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.380
kind of the health
world on certain things,

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.410
but a lot of people don't know

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.270
that I've actually followed your work

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.100
for many, many years,
and we'll get into it.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.410
But it's probably best
coming out of your own mouth.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:16.500
What is the focus of your research?

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.070
And also, what got you into it?

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:22.183
Why do you wanna devote your
life to this area of research?

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.220
- Well, my passion is to extend
healthy lifespan of people.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:31.000
I don't like people getting sick.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.100
And so, I'm a scientist
at Harvard Medical School,

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.480
and we research what I
think is one of the biggest

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.580
biological and medical
problems of all time,

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.400
which is why do we get sick,
especially as we get older.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.750
And we all know that our
bodies are capable of healing,

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.330
especially when we're young,

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.610
and the question is, why is that?

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.850
Why is it when you're young,
you can heal from a wound

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.350
or you don't get cancer or Alzheimer's,

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:55.930
but as you get older, you do?

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.250
And that's what we're
trying to figure out,

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.870
because if we can harness
that energy of youth

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:02.993
and maybe even turn back
the clock in our bodies,

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.170
I think it'll be the biggest
medical revolution of all time.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.640
So, you asked me why do I do it.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.380
Well, you know, I think
like a lot of people

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:12.950
I'd like to leave a legacy.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:14.330
I'd like to have an impact,

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.290
make the world better than I found it.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.360
My grandmother instilled
that in me especially.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.597
But I'm also very curious,

00:03:20.597 --> 00:03:22.390
and I think that trying to tackle

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.670
one of the hardest problems on the planet

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.773
certainly keeps me busy and
very entertained with my lab.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:30.550
- And there's a lot in what you just said.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:31.600
I mean, maybe the first thing

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.791
that I've always found it fascinating is

00:03:33.791 --> 00:03:37.640
we're always caught up with
the diseases of the world,

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:38.630
you know, the big ones.

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:42.000
Cancer, heart disease, diabetes.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.250
But you essentially just
view those as side effects

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.420
of another phenomenon, which is aging.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.600
And what I find fascinating about that is

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.990
aging is such a part of what
it means to be not even human,

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.930
what it means to be a
species as far as we know it,

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:01.590
that we don't even question that

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:05.875
as being the actual cause of
all of these other things.

00:04:05.875 --> 00:04:07.710
When did it click in your mind

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:11.240
that you could address aging itself?

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.160
And at the same time
there's all these other

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.470
you know, Ponce de Leon looking
for the fountain of youth,

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:16.320
and so there's...

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.807
How did you navigate that
kind of fear that, like,

00:04:21.807 --> 00:04:24.047
"Wait, this does seem like
something really important,

00:04:24.047 --> 00:04:25.977
"but if I start talking about aging,

00:04:25.977 --> 00:04:27.677
"people are gonna think I'm Ponce de Leon

00:04:27.677 --> 00:04:30.917
"or one of these folks
looking for eternal life"?

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.090
- Well, it was like that
when I first started.

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:37.640
This is going back to the
late '80s, early '90s.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:39.880
Aging research was the
backwater of biology,

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.690
and to go into it in a serious
way was considered madness.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.387
I had a number of mentors saying,

00:04:45.387 --> 00:04:47.240
"Why would you do such a thing?"

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:49.190
Fortunately, during the 1990s

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:50.870
there were some real big breakthroughs.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:54.010
A geneticist, one of which I worked for,

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:56.030
Lenny Guarente at MIT,

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:57.277
they got into it and said,

00:04:57.277 --> 00:05:00.787
"Okay, let's just throw out
all these ideas why we age.

00:05:00.787 --> 00:05:04.490
"Let's just look for what
genes control aging."

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.790
And what we found as a field

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.940
is that there are individual
genes that you can change

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.230
that dramatically extend
the lifespan of animals,

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:17.030
and yeast cells, which I was
working on as a kid in my 20s.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:20.440
But what that told me and
anyone who would listen

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:24.910
was that the control of
aging is extremely simple.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:26.980
Now, aging of course isn't,

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:31.400
in the same way a car will
wear out for many reasons.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.150
But if there's a way to
slow that whole thing down

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.260
by changing one gene, it means
it's possible to have one day

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.410
a medicine that would slow down

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.380
and perhaps even reverse
aspects of the aging process.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.360
And as you said, Sal, what's
so important about this

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.760
is that we throw away
our preconceived ideas

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.320
about what's natural.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.300
Now, getting heart disease
and cancer 100 years ago

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.340
were natural, and very
little could be done,

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:56.940
and the same is going to be true for aging

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.960
once we accept as a medical profession,

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:01.710
and I'm a scientist not an MD,

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.730
but if we can accept that

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:06.760
one of the main reasons, if
not by far the biggest reason

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.010
that we get these diseases as we get older

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.580
is because of aging.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:12.960
And the reason we haven't made
a lot of progress recently,

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.740
or up until now, is because
too many of us accepted it

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:18.400
as an inevitability.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.440
Now, just to make a point,

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.530
I'm not saying that I'm Ponce de Leon

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:24.860
and I'm gonna give everyone immortality.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.490
That's not the point at all.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.160
And fortunately, our field has

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.510
gone ahead in leaps and bounds,

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:32.673
and now we're one of the most
respected areas of biology.

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:35.710
- Yeah, and (mumbling)

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.860
You mentioned some of
that earlier research

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.320
in Guarente's lab with,
in your case, the yeast.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.200
What are some of what you would say

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.380
the seminal data points
over the last 20 or 30 years

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.067
that have started to
make people realize that,

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:51.270
"Wow, there's a lot here."

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:54.060
And I'm familiar with some of it,

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.730
but I'm guessing a lot
of people don't realize

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.820
what has already been done.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:06:59.653
- Right.

00:06:59.653 --> 00:07:01.770
I'm often shocked when I talk to people

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.550
who are not paying attention,

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.990
because they've got their
own lives to worry about,

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:09.060
how far we've come and
most people don't realize.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:10.110
So, milestones.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:13.010
We've known for, well, close to 100 years

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:16.210
that low-calorie diets can slow down aging

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.890
in animals, and seemingly
in humans as well.

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:22.240
You know, skipping a meal, having a fast,

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:24.653
it's always been considered healthy.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:26.730
We didn't really know why.

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:28.630
And actually, you asked me why was I sure

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:31.310
that studying aging might be helpful.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:33.120
Well, the main reason is that

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:35.580
when you give rats and mice

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:38.180
30% fewer calories in their life,

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:39.020
they live longer.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:41.770
And they don't live longer
because they stay old.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:44.380
They live longer because
they don't get cancer

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:47.740
and these other diseases of
rat aging, or mouse aging.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.610
So, I knew that there was
something to be had there,

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.320
and if diet can do it,

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.360
surely we can make a
medicine to do that as well.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.430
And we've made a lot of
progress towards that.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.600
So, some of the milestones were...

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:03.280
So, the worm C. elegans.
That's a nematode worm.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.700
There was a mutation in that called DAF-2

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:08.080
that extended its lifespan twofold.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.960
And now actually you can
extend the worm by tenfold.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.430
So, that's massive.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:13.263
- You can...

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:17.623
You can extend a worm's
lifespan by a factor of 10.

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:20.190
From gene editing, is that how you do it?

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:22.040
Or is it with some molecule, or both?

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:25.690
- The most powerful ones are genetic,

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:29.740
but there are molecules
that now we can feed a worm,

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:31.742
even a drug that's on the market

00:08:31.742 --> 00:08:36.420
for transplant rejection,
called rapamycin.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:40.660
There are drugs that can
do what fasting, hunger,

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:42.520
used to only be able to do.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:46.250
And another milestone
was we figured out that

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:49.800
changes to how genes are turned
on and off in yeast cells

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.450
contributes to their aging as well.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:55.670
We learnt how to control that in mice.

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:59.550
We can now extend the
lifespan of mice genetically,

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.530
pharmacologically with molecules.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:02.670
And one of the things that...

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.380
Well, there are a couple
of things I wanna say.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:07.530
One is that these genes
are found throughout life.

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:10.190
They're found in yeast cells.
They're found in worms.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:11.450
They're found in mice.
They're found in us.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:13.840
So, we have longevity genes in our body.

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:15.780
And in fact, you can
look at certain variants

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:18.510
and see if you're more
likely to live longer.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:21.700
And then the other big breakthrough
was the realization that

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:23.070
the way we live our lives

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:24.820
and the way animals in
the lab live their lives,

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.160
what they eat, whether they exercise,

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:29.570
if they eat the right foods,

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:30.860
they are actually turning on

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:34.090
these longevity genetic pathways.

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:37.570
So we've finally, I think
we have a model where

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:41.300
the reason that diet and
exercise is so good for us

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.600
isn't just because we stay lean
and because our blood flows.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.330
It's actually because our
bodies have protective genes.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.130
We call these longevity genes.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:53.350
And as we get older, and if
we're lazy and we eat a lot,

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:54.700
they shut off,

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:57.000
but there are ways we
can turn them back on

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.680
by living the right lifestyle,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.770
and hopefully one day
having access to medicines

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:02.133
that turn them on as well.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:06.660
- And what I think the difficult
thing about this research

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:08.220
is obviously you can't study...

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:11.310
It takes too long to study a
human's lifespan. (chuckles)

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:12.620
How do you do that?

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.470
How do you translate the work

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.200
on the worms and the mice and the rats

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:20.643
to something that you
know works in humans?

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:25.040
- Well, there are business tricks to it.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:26.640
What we've done,

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.573
and I've launched a number
of companies to do this,

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:32.210
certainly because a government grant

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:33.820
won't give us enough money to do that.

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:36.290
So the companies, we raise money.

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:40.510
We have traditionally started companies

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:43.010
where we see that these molecules

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:45.690
that could extend lifespan in humans

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:50.690
are also going to be useful
for diseases that need cures.

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:52.070
So, for instance,

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:54.870
there's a company that
is called MetroBiotech.

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:58.010
They work on raising a little molecule

00:10:58.010 --> 00:10:59.960
in the body called NAD,

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:02.750
and it has potential
applications in rare diseases,

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:04.330
such as Friedreich's ataxia,

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:07.820
a genetic disease without a cure,

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:10.780
as well as even potentially
treating COVID-19.

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:14.820
We have a study that's
about to begin in that area,

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:18.130
and we think that that will
boost elderly people's ability

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:21.393
to fight against
infectious disease as well.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:25.760
- And so, just to make sure
we're all on the same page.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:26.960
The way you do this, obviously,

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.100
it'd be very hard to run
a randomized control trial

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:32.410
to see if you can get an average lifespan

00:11:32.410 --> 00:11:34.260
from 80 to 83 years.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:35.500
That research would take forever,

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:37.609
and none of us would be around, maybe,

00:11:37.609 --> 00:11:39.310
to see what happens.

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:41.890
But instead, you look at
some of these diseases

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:44.380
that are associated with aging,

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:46.250
and there you can see, hey,

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.030
if this person gets this type
of therapy, this type of drug,

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:51.120
or this type of lifestyle change,

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.280
what happens to their-

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.330
what happens to those disease conditions.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:57.970
- That's exactly right.

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:01.800
These molecules that I work
on, and other scientists,

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.290
they could be applied to

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:06.620
probably a hundred different diseases

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:08.690
that are related to aging,

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:09.740
and some not.

00:12:09.740 --> 00:12:13.550
Sometimes these are just the
ability to boost the energy

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:16.860
and the repair systems of
even sick young people.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:18.520
So, that's also important to realize

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:20.480
that this research isn't just about

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:22.820
keeping older people around.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:24.920
It's about boosting the
body's natural defenses

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:27.230
against disease and disability

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:29.460
and promoting healing.

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:30.330
And you're right.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:31.530
We can't do

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:35.690
probably a million people over 10 years.

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:38.770
There's no country in the world
that could even afford that.

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:42.470
But the hope is that if we
can get a drug on the market,

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:44.640
or even one that's already on the market,

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.220
and show that if you
look at 5, 10,000 people

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.960
over a few years,

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:52.930
we should be able to see that
they have health improvements,

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:55.400
or at the very least, slowing down of

00:12:56.836 --> 00:12:58.513
the rate of diseases that occur.

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:01.113
And there's a lot of questions about NAD

00:13:01.113 --> 00:13:03.300
that were even coming
before you mentioned it.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:06.130
And for those super fans
of Khan Academy videos

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:07.740
or anyone who has been paying attention

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:09.880
in their freshman biology classes

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:10.870
or their high school biology classes,

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:15.870
you might recognize NAD from
respiration and ATP synthesis.

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.140
So, is that a coincidence?

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:20.220
For those who don't remember,

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:22.750
NAD shows up all the time
in the whole Krebs cycle.

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:25.370
So, explain. Is there a connection?

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:27.810
- Yeah, so, NAD is this little molecule

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:30.000
that cells use to carry out,

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.620
oh, about 400 or more enzymatic reactions.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:34.110
It's one of the most boring molecules,

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:36.820
or at least it was up until the 2000s.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:39.050
But what my colleagues and I realized

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:41.520
was that NAD levels also control

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:44.070
the body's survival networks.

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.470
And the lower your NAD levels, we believe,

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.560
the lower your defenses are.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:50.230
And actually, as we get older,

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:53.170
and certainly, we know for sure
in animals as they get older

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:54.830
they have less NAD.

00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:57.840
You can boost your NAD
levels somewhat back up

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:02.840
by doing exercise and skipping
a meal maybe once a day.

00:14:02.860 --> 00:14:05.610
That is fairly well accepted.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:08.130
But you can't expect
someone who's in a hospital

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:10.350
or who's elderly or sick

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:13.970
to go for a run easily, or skip meals.

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:18.010
So, what we need are
potentially NAD boosters.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:21.770
This is a group of drugs that
I'm working on intensively.

00:14:21.770 --> 00:14:24.190
And we're hopeful that
the same kind of things

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:26.270
that we see in mice in the lab,

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:27.780
some of them will happen in people.

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:30.980
And we'll probably know
in the next year or two,

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:33.440
or maybe sooner with the COVID-19 study.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:34.440
But the kind of things we see

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:37.390
when we raise NAD levels
back up in old mice,

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:38.410
well, there's a lot of studies

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:39.810
way beyond what my lab has done,

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:41.430
but some of the things that I know of

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:43.330
from my lab are increased endurance.

00:14:43.330 --> 00:14:47.310
So, old mice run farther. They
have much better blood flow.

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:49.270
They have better blood
flow in the brain as well,

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:51.003
not just their skeletal muscle.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:53.880
They're resistant to heart attacks.

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:55.470
They have less inflammation.

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:58.580
Their respiration and their
mitochondria get revved up

00:14:58.580 --> 00:15:00.380
like a young animal would.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:02.630
So, there are lots of benefits
that we can see in mice.

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:04.660
We have a longevity
study that's in progress

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:05.780
that's looking promising.

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:07.920
I can't say much more just yet

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:10.240
because people in my lab will kill me.

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:14.450
But these are the kinda
things that hold out promise

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:17.260
that we can use some of
these changes in animals

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:18.980
to treat patients,

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:22.880
and ultimately, if a
drug gets on the market

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:24.870
for a particular disease,

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:27.170
my hope, my personal hope is that

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:30.490
now that drug can be tested
in many different areas,

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:33.120
many different disease areas by doctors,

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.400
and do their own clinical trials

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:35.950
in their own particular area.

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:38.400
And eventually, if everything goes well,

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:40.840
we could have millions or
tens of millions of people

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:43.100
being treated with these
drugs for not just diseases,

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:46.750
but eventually we'll see
that those people on the drug

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:49.610
hopefully will be less prone to diseases

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:50.860
and may even live longer.

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:54.400
- Yeah. And there's a ton of
questions here from Twitter.

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:57.247
AZ Latina, "Please ask Dr. Sinclair

00:15:57.247 --> 00:15:58.927
"regarding the status of his research

00:15:58.927 --> 00:16:02.700
"on NAD neurodegenerative diseases."

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:03.870
I don't know if that's the research

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:06.300
that your staff would kill
you for talking about.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:10.083
Other people are talking
about NAD infusions.

00:16:10.083 --> 00:16:11.860
And I'm gonna give a disclaimer here.

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:13.970
This is not a place to
get any medical advice.

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:17.560
This is not a place for any of that.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:18.640
We're just having a conversation.

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:21.950
Please consult doctors,
et cetera, standard.

00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:23.560
And then there's people
talking about other molecules

00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:25.450
which I know you have done research on,

00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:26.730
things like resveratrol,

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:29.330
or you know people who've
done research on resveratrol,

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:30.550
stair- I can never say it.

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:33.680
I know the molecule,
pterostilbene. (chuckles)

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:36.507
It starts with a P, pterostilbene.

00:16:36.507 --> 00:16:38.963
You know how to pronounce
it better than I do.

00:16:39.910 --> 00:16:41.950
So, I'll just group all of those.

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.190
Once again, I don't wanna
make any recommendations

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:46.120
of what people should or
shouldn't do with their lives.

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:48.310
People should do their own research.

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:50.900
But, I mean, you
mentioned NAD. How does...

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:52.853
I guess, if you could,
talk about what you know

00:16:52.853 --> 00:16:55.830
about NAD and neurodegenerative diseases.

00:16:55.830 --> 00:16:59.810
And also, how do you change
NAD levels in, say, a mouse,

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:01.853
or in, potentially, in human beings?

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:06.500
- Well, in mice there are a couple of ways

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:08.500
that NAD seems to work.

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:09.940
It will raise the levels

00:17:11.189 --> 00:17:14.040
or activity of a set of
genes which we call sirtuins,

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:17.390
and many of those have been
show to be neuroprotective,

00:17:17.390 --> 00:17:18.223
again in mice.

00:17:18.223 --> 00:17:21.200
Now, you know, it's easy to
criticize these mouse studies,

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:23.950
but in defense, we have
to start somewhere.

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:25.530
But yeah, there's a lot of data

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:27.670
from other labs in particular,
a little bit from mine,

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:29.750
that you'll get neuroprotection

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:33.210
in a variety of diseases
from sirtuin activity,

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:35.370
from Parkinson's through to Alzheimer's.

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:36.990
And the second thing that happens,

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:38.640
which I mentioned earlier,

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:42.750
is that the mice get more
blood flow in their brain,

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:45.720
and we tracked that down to
the lining of blood vessels

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.850
actually become more youthful and grow,

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:51.650
in a healthy way, nothing
dangerous that we can tell.

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:54.110
And that makes me think
that there might be a chance

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:57.780
to use these so-called
NAD-boosting molecules

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:02.290
to treat vascular dementia,
which is a very pernicious

00:18:04.269 --> 00:18:07.720
and common disease, actually,
with not much you can do.

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:10.010
So, I think that there's a lot of reason

00:18:10.010 --> 00:18:12.440
to go and explore NAD boosters

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:14.680
in neurodegenerative diseases.

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:16.800
I haven't done any myself in humans.

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:19.790
I'm aware that there are some studies.

00:18:19.790 --> 00:18:22.010
There's one in Parkinson's.

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:25.110
There's one, I believe it was also in ALS,

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:27.690
which I know is not neurodegenerative,

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:29.680
central but peripheral.

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:32.660
But yeah, I think that that's one area.

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:36.400
The area that I've more
particularly worked on

00:18:36.400 --> 00:18:41.400
are muscle wasting
diseases, and also immunity.

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:44.300
- And what is...

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:48.230
How do you adjust the NAD
levels in a mouse or a human?

00:18:48.230 --> 00:18:50.560
Is it an injection? Is it a pill?

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:54.215
Is it genetic? How do you do that?

00:18:54.215 --> 00:18:57.260
- I have to be careful because
I'm not prescribing anything.

00:18:57.260 --> 00:18:59.483
I'm just a PhD talking about science.

00:19:00.430 --> 00:19:02.100
Let's make that clear.

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:05.370
But there are molecules
you can give to a mouse.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:07.860
They're called NAD precursors.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:09.700
These are the building blocks of NAD.

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:12.880
And we know from mouse
and some human studies

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:14.870
that I've been involved in

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:18.260
that they will raise
NAD levels in the body.

00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:19.950
And the hope is that by doing that

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.340
we'll get those sirtuin defenses back up

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.230
to where they used to
be when you were young.

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:27.390
So, you can take pills. That's sufficient.

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:28.590
Some people do injections.

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:33.260
I don't know if they work or
not, but I hear about them.

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:34.760
But based on the literature,

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:36.550
we're right on the cusp of learning

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:40.270
what these NAD boosters are
capable of doing in people.

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:42.220
And many of them are natural molecules,

00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:45.483
so that piques people's interest as well.

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:47.470
- Yeah.

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:50.170
You can imagine there's a
lot of questions about this.

00:19:51.852 --> 00:19:53.550
Well, actually, there's some philosophical

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:54.520
and there's some medical questions.

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:56.540
So, let's go to the medical ones first.

00:19:56.540 --> 00:20:01.100
So, Madonna Chicotta from Facebook,

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:02.460
she's 60 years old.

00:20:02.460 --> 00:20:03.967
She's saying, "How many
years before we see

00:20:03.967 --> 00:20:07.480
"a cocktail of drugs that extend lives?"

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:09.750
So, obviously, no one
can predict the future,

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:11.730
but you're really are the forefront

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:12.680
of where this research is.

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:13.800
What do you think things
are gonna look like

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:14.740
in 5 or 10 years?

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:16.500
When do you think this is
going to become very relevant

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:20.023
to the average person
watching us right now?

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:24.513
- There are, at last estimate,

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:28.200
20 companies who are either close to

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:32.560
or running clinical trials that
came out of aging research.

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:34.780
I'm hopeful that one or more of them

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:37.420
will be successful in the coming years.

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:39.710
How long until we have a cocktail?

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:41.390
It's probably more than that.

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:43.290
Drug development, as
I've learnt in my career,

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:47.120
is extremely tough, and
plenty of things can stop you.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:49.770
So I'm always a bit cautious

00:20:49.770 --> 00:20:52.520
about making predictions for the future.

00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:55.030
There are drugs, actually,
that are on the market.

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:56.550
One I mentioned, which is rapamycin,

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:58.230
which is not something you'd take lightly

00:20:58.230 --> 00:20:59.500
because it has some toxicity.

00:20:59.500 --> 00:21:02.720
But there's another one, for
example, called metformin,

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:06.490
which anybody, almost
anybody who has type 2

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:09.360
or age-associated diabetes will know of.

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:11.310
It's also called Glucophage,

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:13.720
which lowers, tends to lower blood sugar.

00:21:13.720 --> 00:21:18.200
It's one of the safer drugs
that you can get anywhere,

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:19.650
and it's also on the list of-

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:24.080
on WHO's list of essential
medicines for humanity.

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:25.890
So, why do I mention that?

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:29.173
Because if you feed it to
animals, they're healthier.

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:31.220
Mice can live longer.

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:33.480
And also, in studies that have looked at

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:36.340
10s of thousands of people on this drug,

00:21:36.340 --> 00:21:39.410
those people on average tend
to be not just resistant

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:41.860
or doing better for type 2 diabetes,

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:44.720
but other diseases like
cancer, heart disease,

00:21:44.720 --> 00:21:46.910
Alzheimer's, and even frailty.

00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:49.040
It seems that those people
on the drug actually

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:52.480
have less chance than somebody
that never takes the drug.

00:21:52.480 --> 00:21:54.740
So, the reason I mention that is that

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:56.620
there may be drugs already out there

00:21:56.620 --> 00:21:59.950
that are going to be
part of a future cocktail

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:02.430
that people can go on in their mid-life,

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:03.800
you know, under doctor supervision

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:05.700
to make sure it's all safe.

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:07.150
I don't think we're that far away.

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:09.600
In terms of the science,
we're pretty much there.

00:22:11.004 --> 00:22:12.620
- I know you're not a futurist,

00:22:12.620 --> 00:22:14.080
but given your area of research

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:15.550
I'm sure people ask you questions,

00:22:15.550 --> 00:22:18.119
so I will ask you a little
bit more about that.

00:22:18.119 --> 00:22:19.490
When do...

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:22.170
I mean, do you think that in 30 years

00:22:22.170 --> 00:22:24.240
we could be talking about a human lifespan

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:28.193
that is appreciably longer,
5, 10, 15 years longer?

00:22:29.876 --> 00:22:32.853
- Yeah, I'd be surprised
if there wasn't a gain.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:37.053
We live in a time where the
average lifespan is going down.

00:22:38.010 --> 00:22:40.560
That's tragic and difficult.

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.700
But in 30 years when we're
over this current crisis

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:45.970
and this research that I'm talking about

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:48.530
and new areas that are emerging

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:51.820
have blossomed and given us medicines,

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:56.810
yeah, I think five years
is not that challenging.

00:22:56.810 --> 00:22:57.910
Making it to 50 years,

00:22:57.910 --> 00:23:00.890
you know, another 50
years, is much harder.

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:03.530
But there's some new
technologies that are exciting

00:23:03.530 --> 00:23:07.432
that are complementary to this NAD work.

00:23:07.432 --> 00:23:09.042
There's a way to delete old cells

00:23:09.042 --> 00:23:12.030
and hurtful cells in the body.

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:14.260
These are called senescent cells.

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:16.000
There are some human
trials that look promising,

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:17.650
out of Mayo Clinic, for example.

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:18.560
- How do they do that?

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:21.830
How do they delete the senescent cells?

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:23.730
I've read about the issue
with senescent cells.

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:26.560
I know things like fasting
can help with the autophagy.

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:28.393
But how do you do that in a trial?

00:23:30.100 --> 00:23:32.340
- Well, scientists have
looked for molecules

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:35.800
that either singly, or
typically in combination,

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:38.140
will kill a senescent cell but not hurt

00:23:38.140 --> 00:23:40.530
the natural other healthy cells.

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:42.430
And the problem is that
those senescent cells

00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:45.470
are just stuck in a zombie-like state

00:23:45.470 --> 00:23:47.760
and you need to trigger
their death process.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:50.090
So that's what those molecules do.

00:23:50.090 --> 00:23:52.190
And when you do that, in a mouse at least,

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:54.550
they're a lot healthier.

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:57.583
And you don't need a lot of
senescent cells to cause havoc.

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:00.460
Jim Kirkland from Mayo Clinic

00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:03.550
put just a dab of senescent
cells into a mouse

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:06.320
and it had more signs of aging.

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:08.160
So, that's one way.

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:09.790
The other area that's of interest

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:13.820
is actual reprogramming of
the body to be young again.

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:15.370
Not just slowing down aging,

00:24:15.370 --> 00:24:18.830
but literally turning back
the clock of the body.

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:21.590
And the reason I bring up
that one in particular,

00:24:21.590 --> 00:24:23.670
besides that we work on it,

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:26.440
is that that to me is a game changer.

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:29.320
If that works and it's safe in humans,

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:31.400
you could potentially roll back the clock

00:24:31.400 --> 00:24:33.013
a decade in the body.

00:24:34.330 --> 00:24:35.520
- What does that mean?

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:36.940
Is that...

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:40.470
I know decades ago people
talked a lot about telomeres,

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:42.890
which are kind of those, what
we thought were nonsense DNA

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:46.193
at the end of chromosomes.

00:24:47.903 --> 00:24:49.750
What mechanisms are you talking about

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:52.350
when you talk about actually
turning back the clock?

00:24:53.540 --> 00:24:56.400
- Yeah, so, I wrote a book about this

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:59.490
if anyone wants to follow
what I'm talking about.

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:01.160
Essentially, it's the idea that-

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:02.243
- I have a copy.

00:25:03.250 --> 00:25:04.660
Right there.

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:08.340
- Thank you.
(Sal chuckles)

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:12.650
So, this was 20 years of my thinking

00:25:12.650 --> 00:25:14.420
all rolled into one book.

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:18.990
And the idea, besides tips
on how to live longer,

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:21.660
the idea is a novel one, which is that

00:25:21.660 --> 00:25:25.250
aging is primarily a loss
of information in the body

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:28.180
and that our old cells
just lose the ability

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:30.670
to read the right genes at the right time.

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:32.190
And we've known this occurs,

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:34.730
but very few people have thought

00:25:34.730 --> 00:25:37.640
that that's what's driving
all these other problems.

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:39.910
And so, we've done experiments in my lab,

00:25:39.910 --> 00:25:42.250
some of which we're about to publish,

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:44.920
which say that you can
cause aging to go forwards

00:25:44.920 --> 00:25:47.500
by disrupting that information,

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:49.433
and then we can also reprogram cells

00:25:49.433 --> 00:25:52.481
that get that information back again.

00:25:52.481 --> 00:25:54.150
That's a game changer.

00:25:54.150 --> 00:25:57.270
How is it possible to
restore the age of a cell?

00:25:57.270 --> 00:25:59.380
Isn't that information gone forever?

00:25:59.380 --> 00:26:02.660
If you break a compact disc or a DVD,

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:04.710
how are you possibly gonna
put that back together?

00:26:04.710 --> 00:26:06.660
But what we're seeing in the lab

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:09.360
is that if you just turn
on some very specific genes

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:11.210
that are normally only on

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:14.770
when you're very, very young, in the womb,

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:17.713
the cells have the ability
to reset their age.

00:26:18.620 --> 00:26:22.680
Not back to zero, but far
enough that the cells now

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:24.720
are behaving like they're young again.

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:26.600
And literally, when you measure their age,

00:26:26.600 --> 00:26:27.933
and we have a way of doing that now,

00:26:27.933 --> 00:26:30.370
there's a clock we can
measure in the cell,

00:26:30.370 --> 00:26:31.920
they are literally young again.

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:37.740
- And I mean, that is, to
put it lightly, mind-blowing,

00:26:37.740 --> 00:26:41.030
and I know you wanna speak
about it in very tempered ways

00:26:41.030 --> 00:26:46.030
because it leads to very big
possibilities or questions,

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:48.963
many of which people have
been asking on social media.

00:26:50.006 --> 00:26:53.240
Let's put our science fiction hat on.

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:54.890
Let's say that that works,

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:59.230
that that, you know, in 10,
15 years there are therapies

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:02.160
that go along the lines
that you are saying.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:04.460
I'm almost afraid to
say what it could imply

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:07.883
because people would
think that I'm dreaming.

00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:10.890
But in a world where you could,

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:12.540
I mean, I guess, you know, keeps-

00:27:13.814 --> 00:27:16.330
You know, a 70-year-old who
looks like a 30-year-old

00:27:16.330 --> 00:27:17.630
type of scenario,

00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:21.163
and then therefore could
live much, much longer.

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:25.490
There's all these philosophical
questions people ask about.

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:27.677
From YouTube, Vivez Speaker says,

00:27:27.677 --> 00:27:29.527
"If nobody aged, what
will happen to the earth?

00:27:29.527 --> 00:27:31.210
"Will we have to build more land?"

00:27:31.210 --> 00:27:32.897
Samara from Facebook is saying,

00:27:32.897 --> 00:27:34.347
"I would be so worried about medication

00:27:34.347 --> 00:27:35.837
"that increases longevity.

00:27:35.837 --> 00:27:38.697
"We'd be tampering with
the circle of life."

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:41.410
I'm sure you get a lot of that.

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:43.800
How do you view it?

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:45.410
Or do you think this creates problems,

00:27:45.410 --> 00:27:47.610
or this creates solutions,
or we don't know?

00:27:48.880 --> 00:27:52.050
- Well, yeah, the last part
of my book talks about this,

00:27:52.050 --> 00:27:55.160
about the good and the
bad that may come of this.

00:27:55.160 --> 00:27:58.780
Now, the good, I think is, to me, obvious.

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:00.850
We're hoping to do our
first clinical trial

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:02.620
in under two years from now

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:05.050
to turn back the age of the eye.

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:07.150
And in mice, it's pretty easy.

00:28:07.150 --> 00:28:09.670
We can turn back the age
of an old eye in a mouse

00:28:09.670 --> 00:28:11.570
and restore its vision.

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:14.120
We can treat glaucoma,
pressure in the eye.

00:28:14.120 --> 00:28:16.780
We can treat even optic nerve damage.

00:28:16.780 --> 00:28:18.170
So, there are parts of the body

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:20.330
that I think will initially benefit.

00:28:20.330 --> 00:28:24.380
And if it's your sister, or
your child, or your parent

00:28:24.380 --> 00:28:26.610
who's been injured or has a disease,

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:28.377
I think that you'd be hard pressed to say,

00:28:28.377 --> 00:28:30.127
"Oh, don't give them a medicine.

00:28:30.127 --> 00:28:32.880
"It's the circle of
life. Let them suffer."

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:34.510
That's the easy part.

00:28:34.510 --> 00:28:37.200
But if you wanna get into the ethics,

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:39.340
if we really are able
to turn back the clock

00:28:39.340 --> 00:28:42.310
10, 20, 30 years in people,

00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:44.840
yeah, what does the world look like?

00:28:44.840 --> 00:28:46.440
And that's a very important question.

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:49.040
So, some of the work that I've
been doing is with economists

00:28:49.040 --> 00:28:53.700
to see what happens as people live longer.

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:55.470
The important thing about
the economy I should mention

00:28:55.470 --> 00:28:59.770
is that when people are old
and sick, it's a huge drain.

00:28:59.770 --> 00:29:00.770
You know, in the U.S. we're spending

00:29:00.770 --> 00:29:02.480
17% of GDP on healthcare,

00:29:02.480 --> 00:29:04.840
and that's mostly because of the elderly.

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:07.610
If older people are still
productive and healthy

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:09.600
and then die fairly rapidly,

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:10.980
that's a massive saving,

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:13.647
in the trillions of dollars, for the U.S.

00:29:13.647 --> 00:29:15.970
within even just the first decade,

00:29:15.970 --> 00:29:17.510
and global economy would thrive.

00:29:17.510 --> 00:29:20.550
So, economically, it's not going to

00:29:20.550 --> 00:29:22.410
be the big issue that you might think.

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:25.070
And actually, also, you
mentioned population.

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:26.690
If you do the numbers,

00:29:26.690 --> 00:29:29.020
sure, if nobody ever
dies, that's a problem,

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:32.350
and I wouldn't even say that's
something I'm trying to do.

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:34.680
But am I trying to give people

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:36.180
more health and prevent suffering?

00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:37.280
Absolutely.

00:29:37.280 --> 00:29:40.150
And actually, even if we
stopped anyone dying today,

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:41.270
the rate of population growth

00:29:41.270 --> 00:29:43.230
would be less than immigration in the U.S.

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:46.170
So, it's not going to ruin
the world any time soon.

00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:48.500
We have bigger problems
to worry about than that.

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:52.520
And I think by saving
these trillions of dollars,

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:54.720
that's money that can
be put towards solving

00:29:56.420 --> 00:29:58.470
the problems that we really
need to solve right now

00:29:58.470 --> 00:30:02.393
in housing, unemployment,
and climate change.

00:30:03.350 --> 00:30:04.680
- Right, and you mentioned it in the book,

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:07.980
but the kind of crowding
issue is much more of a

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:11.030
not only just lifespan,
but there's reproduction,

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:13.880
and there's good argument that
as people become wealthier

00:30:14.789 --> 00:30:16.260
and more economically productive,

00:30:16.260 --> 00:30:17.460
those are the countries
that are actually having

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:18.683
shrinking populations.

00:30:20.743 --> 00:30:23.100
So yeah, it's a fascinating...

00:30:23.100 --> 00:30:24.700
What is your...

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:26.480
Well, I'm sure people...

00:30:26.480 --> 00:30:30.668
Well, I don't know if
you mind me saying this.

00:30:30.668 --> 00:30:32.710
For those who don't know, my wife and I,

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:34.250
my wife, Umaima, and I met David

00:30:34.250 --> 00:30:37.080
at a conference a couple of years ago.

00:30:37.080 --> 00:30:38.860
And I had been following
his work for a long time,

00:30:38.860 --> 00:30:39.830
so I was very excited to meet him.

00:30:39.830 --> 00:30:40.853
We started chatting.

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:46.120
And I know this is kind of a
flip dimension of all of this.

00:30:46.120 --> 00:30:47.750
You're a serious researcher.

00:30:47.750 --> 00:30:49.690
But you're clearly doing something right.

00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:51.260
Anyone looking at the screen right now

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:54.470
would assume that I'm like
10 years older than you.

00:30:54.470 --> 00:30:55.303
- No.

00:30:55.303 --> 00:30:58.710
- But I think you're 10
years older than me. (laughs)

00:30:58.710 --> 00:31:01.350
- Well, I just turned 51, but
I haven't shaved in a while

00:31:01.350 --> 00:31:02.980
and haven't had a haircut in a while.

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:04.723
But thank you for saying that.

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:06.920
Yeah, I mean, we'll see.

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:08.180
I think the proof is in the pudding,

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:10.120
and I'm trying to

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:13.000
live the life that I
think is the healthiest,

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:14.133
as a role model.

00:31:15.029 --> 00:31:18.150
If I didn't do what I'm
telling others to do,

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:19.950
it would be hypocritical.

00:31:19.950 --> 00:31:22.170
So I try to exercise. I try to eat well.

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:24.320
I try to, if I can, skip breakfast

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:25.723
and even have a late lunch.

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:29.803
My father does something
very similar, and we'll see.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:33.000
Neither of us is sick at all.

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:35.990
My father's 80, close to 81,

00:31:35.990 --> 00:31:38.050
and as vibrant as ever.

00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:39.380
I think he's even stronger than me

00:31:39.380 --> 00:31:41.880
the last time we checked.

00:31:41.880 --> 00:31:43.470
But you know, I'm not
trying to prove anything.

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:45.360
I'm not worried about dying myself.

00:31:45.360 --> 00:31:47.010
I'm just curious to see what happens,

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:48.510
and I'm measuring things in myself

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:51.503
to try and gain insights into
what works and what doesn't.

00:31:52.620 --> 00:31:53.640
- And this is, and look,

00:31:53.640 --> 00:31:55.990
I'll give the same disclaimer
I've given several times.

00:31:55.990 --> 00:31:57.550
Neither of us are doctors.

00:31:57.550 --> 00:31:59.270
Neither of us are trying to give advice

00:31:59.270 --> 00:32:02.290
or sell pharmaceuticals, et cetera.

00:32:02.290 --> 00:32:03.270
But I am curious.

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:04.840
I mean, you mentioned you
do a few things, actually,

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:06.230
and we have that in common.

00:32:06.230 --> 00:32:08.570
I also do intermittent fasting.

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:11.633
As we speak, I haven't
eaten for about 20 years.

00:32:13.505 --> 00:32:14.770
So, I do that.

00:32:14.770 --> 00:32:17.720
I've actually recently become pescatarian.

00:32:17.720 --> 00:32:18.880
I like fish a lot,

00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:21.530
so that's taking me a little
bit harder to let go of.

00:32:22.650 --> 00:32:25.500
So, I try to do a lot of these
things, and you're right.

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:27.870
Regardless of what it does to
lifespan, I just feel better.

00:32:27.870 --> 00:32:28.740
I feel healthier.

00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:30.708
I have much more mental
clarity during the day,

00:32:30.708 --> 00:32:32.460
actually in a fasted state.

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:34.000
Once again, not medical advice.

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:35.280
Don't do it while you're growing, kids,

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:36.710
but when you're an adult,
you can think about it.

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:38.570
Read the research. Decide for yourself.

00:32:38.570 --> 00:32:41.040
What other things do you,

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:43.350
I mean, or at least would
you recommend people research

00:32:43.350 --> 00:32:45.760
and make a decision for
themselves if it makes sense,

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:46.593
or you know other people
in the field that think,

00:32:46.593 --> 00:32:48.547
"Yeah, this is probably a good idea.

00:32:48.547 --> 00:32:50.197
"It might make sense."

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:55.040
- Well, yeah, the exercise
is the other thing

00:32:55.040 --> 00:32:57.680
besides eating less.

00:32:57.680 --> 00:32:58.520
And again, you know,

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:00.880
we're not talking about
malnutrition, starvation.

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:03.380
No. You need a healthy diet.

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:06.040
And you can make up for
it with your other meals.

00:33:06.040 --> 00:33:08.650
But what we find in
animals, and in humans,

00:33:08.650 --> 00:33:10.170
is that being hungry for
a little part of the day

00:33:10.170 --> 00:33:11.650
turns on these defenses.

00:33:11.650 --> 00:33:13.750
But exercise, let's turn to that.

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:16.280
Right in front of me, you
can't see this, anybody,

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:19.723
but I have a gym, which
is attached to my bedroom.

00:33:22.030 --> 00:33:23.470
I'm sure-
- That's hardcore.

00:33:23.470 --> 00:33:26.400
You have a gym attached to your bedroom.

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:28.780
- I do. I should use it more often.

00:33:28.780 --> 00:33:31.090
But yeah, I do. I do try to workout.

00:33:31.090 --> 00:33:33.250
I've got a treadmill. I've got weights.

00:33:33.250 --> 00:33:34.280
And looking at my father,

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:36.910
who's been doing this for
many more years than I have,

00:33:36.910 --> 00:33:40.280
it's essential for health in your old age.

00:33:40.280 --> 00:33:42.530
And it's not just about
maintaining your muscle mass,

00:33:42.530 --> 00:33:45.240
which is, of course, you
lose that as you get older.

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:46.310
It's also flexibility.

00:33:46.310 --> 00:33:48.430
So if you fall over, you'll bounce,

00:33:48.430 --> 00:33:51.040
and you also won't tear anything,

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:52.120
and that's just as important.

00:33:52.120 --> 00:33:53.730
So, keep that flexibility.

00:33:53.730 --> 00:33:55.830
Keep the muscle strength up.

00:33:55.830 --> 00:33:58.760
But also, if you exercise just for,

00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:00.350
it's been shown just for 10 minutes a day,

00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:01.610
lose your breath,

00:34:01.610 --> 00:34:04.560
that will also activate
these longevity pathways,

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:06.500
these genes that I talk about.

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:07.720
And so, it doesn't take that much.

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:09.650
You don't need to be a marathon runner

00:34:09.650 --> 00:34:11.923
to get a lot of the benefits
we're talking about.

00:34:12.960 --> 00:34:14.240
- And I've read about that as well,

00:34:14.240 --> 00:34:15.860
and actually, a lot of trainers these days

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:18.590
advocate it with these
high-intensity training.

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:19.750
What you just said was interesting.

00:34:19.750 --> 00:34:20.720
You said lose your breath.

00:34:20.720 --> 00:34:22.690
That's kind of what you see
as one of the key factors?

00:34:22.690 --> 00:34:25.021
Is there some evidence
that when you get winded,

00:34:25.021 --> 00:34:27.233
that stimulates some of these pathways?

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:30.703
- Right, that seems to be the case.

00:34:33.120 --> 00:34:34.570
Just walking will be good for you,

00:34:34.570 --> 00:34:37.240
but yeah, if you can just get hypoxia,

00:34:37.240 --> 00:34:38.950
going to lack of oxygen,

00:34:38.950 --> 00:34:40.500
then you stimulate all sorts of things,

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:43.790
like boosting those
energy-producing mitochondria.

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:46.670
And it's borne out in
studies of people who,

00:34:46.670 --> 00:34:49.190
particularly elderly people get studied.

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:52.440
There's a great place down
in University of Florida

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:55.040
where they test what's
good for older people,

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:57.350
and it is very clear
that losing your breath

00:34:57.350 --> 00:34:58.520
is the best thing.

00:34:58.520 --> 00:35:01.000
I don't find it pleasant,
losing my breath.

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:03.140
Some people do, but I'm not in that camp.

00:35:03.140 --> 00:35:05.880
So, I push sleds when I'm at the gym.

00:35:05.880 --> 00:35:07.653
I try to run a little bit.

00:35:07.653 --> 00:35:10.543
That's the least you should do, actually.

00:35:11.800 --> 00:35:13.500
- I just find these conversations-

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:15.000
Oh, we're already over time,
but I have one last question.

00:35:15.000 --> 00:35:17.070
Because, obviously we're
having this conversation

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:18.350
about health and aging

00:35:18.350 --> 00:35:20.060
in a time where there's a global pandemic.

00:35:20.060 --> 00:35:21.040
And you touched on it a little bit

00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:24.710
that molecules like NAD might
have a benefit in COVID.

00:35:24.710 --> 00:35:28.400
And COVID is an example that
has clear age correlations,

00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:32.770
where as far as I know there's
zero or a handful of cases

00:35:32.770 --> 00:35:36.450
of children being
seriously sick from COVID,

00:35:36.450 --> 00:35:41.280
and the risks just go up
exponentially as you get older.

00:35:41.280 --> 00:35:43.200
What do you understand?

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:45.270
How much have you directly studied COVID?

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:46.180
What do you understand about it?

00:35:46.180 --> 00:35:48.330
Why do you think it has such
a strong age correlation?

00:35:48.330 --> 00:35:50.380
Essentially, kids don't get it,

00:35:50.380 --> 00:35:52.730
or they get it, but they
don't have any of the real,

00:35:52.730 --> 00:35:53.993
the bad symptoms.

00:35:54.870 --> 00:35:55.703
- Yeah.

00:35:55.703 --> 00:35:57.040
So, I wrote a review on this

00:35:57.040 --> 00:35:59.170
which I think is fairly
available to anybody.

00:35:59.170 --> 00:36:01.240
So, if you google my name,

00:36:01.240 --> 00:36:03.170
you should be able to read about it.

00:36:03.170 --> 00:36:05.353
There are lots of explanations,
or potential explanations.

00:36:05.353 --> 00:36:07.630
I don't think anybody really knows.

00:36:07.630 --> 00:36:08.920
There's everything from changes

00:36:08.920 --> 00:36:11.200
to how proteins are modified,

00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:14.920
through to exhaustion of our
immune system as we get older.

00:36:14.920 --> 00:36:16.660
Even having lots of viral infections

00:36:16.660 --> 00:36:18.703
during our lifetime can exhaust that.

00:36:19.550 --> 00:36:23.157
Part of the possible
explanation does include NAD.

00:36:24.210 --> 00:36:26.900
A lot of people probably roll their eyes.

00:36:26.900 --> 00:36:29.730
You know, "Can Sinclair talk
about anything else but NAD?"

00:36:29.730 --> 00:36:31.920
And during the beginning of the pandemic,

00:36:31.920 --> 00:36:36.920
I resisted really trying to
figure out if NAD had a role,

00:36:37.090 --> 00:36:38.520
but more and more evidence came up.

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:41.660
So, we've had a study in my
lab for the last few years

00:36:41.660 --> 00:36:44.370
that shows that NAD depletion occurs,

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:47.190
a lack of NAD occurs when
there's an infection.

00:36:47.190 --> 00:36:48.240
That's one bit of evidence.

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:51.610
But then there was another
study, Charlie Brenner,

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:53.260
a professor showed that

00:36:53.260 --> 00:36:55.510
patient samples actually have low NAD

00:36:55.510 --> 00:36:57.610
if they have bad COVID infection.

00:36:57.610 --> 00:36:59.220
And then there are some case studies

00:36:59.220 --> 00:37:02.240
coming out of Cedars-Sinai Hospital

00:37:02.240 --> 00:37:04.470
with patients that did remarkably well

00:37:04.470 --> 00:37:06.890
given an NAD booster cocktail.

00:37:06.890 --> 00:37:10.240
So, all of that led me to
look more and more into it.

00:37:10.240 --> 00:37:13.120
And it turns out that my lab,

00:37:13.120 --> 00:37:15.710
and now Charlie's lab has also shown

00:37:15.710 --> 00:37:19.680
that the virus is
depleting NAD in the body.

00:37:19.680 --> 00:37:21.420
It does that for reasons
we're not really sure,

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:25.640
but it might be part of the
pathogenesis, as we call it,

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:28.040
part of the problem that the virus causes.

00:37:28.040 --> 00:37:30.298
And so the hope, and
we're testing this now,

00:37:30.298 --> 00:37:32.410
hopefully soon in patients,

00:37:32.410 --> 00:37:37.360
is that by getting that NAD
depletion back up to normal

00:37:37.360 --> 00:37:39.750
that elderly people will have the energy

00:37:39.750 --> 00:37:42.200
and the immune system to
be able to fight the virus.

00:37:42.200 --> 00:37:43.560
And there's another
thing that's interesting,

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:45.190
is that that hyperinflammation

00:37:45.190 --> 00:37:48.600
where these severe cases actually succumb

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:53.600
to over-inflammation,
or the cytokine storm,

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:56.380
that we know that if you have less NAD,

00:37:56.380 --> 00:37:58.117
you get this hyperactivation.

00:37:58.117 --> 00:38:01.500
And so, that's another
possible way that NAD boosting

00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:04.320
could actually help
people with a very extreme

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:06.203
late stage form of COVID-19.

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:08.900
- So, that's interesting,

00:38:08.900 --> 00:38:10.990
because when you see the
cytokine storm, as you mentioned,

00:38:10.990 --> 00:38:13.580
that's when inflammation,
that's your immune system,

00:38:13.580 --> 00:38:15.640
is going and attacking a bunch of stuff.

00:38:15.640 --> 00:38:20.590
Cytokines are cells in your immune system.

00:38:20.590 --> 00:38:24.280
And that's one of the
causes of severe illness

00:38:24.280 --> 00:38:26.450
or even death of COVID.

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:28.256
It's actually because...

00:38:28.256 --> 00:38:30.650
It's associated when people have low NAD,

00:38:30.650 --> 00:38:32.480
they're more likely to
have a cytokine storm.

00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:34.060
That seems counterintuitive,

00:38:34.060 --> 00:38:35.360
because I always associate NAD

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:39.870
with ATP production and metabolism,

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:41.420
but that you can be overactive.

00:38:43.130 --> 00:38:44.963
Is my intuition off, or?

00:38:46.130 --> 00:38:49.240
- Well, the critical determinant of this

00:38:49.240 --> 00:38:50.340
is called the inflammasome,

00:38:50.340 --> 00:38:54.090
and there's a protein called NLRP3,

00:38:54.090 --> 00:38:55.750
and a couple of labs have shown

00:38:55.750 --> 00:38:58.350
that NAD levels directly control

00:38:58.350 --> 00:39:00.380
the activity of that protein

00:39:00.380 --> 00:39:02.810
and that if you don't have enough NAD,

00:39:02.810 --> 00:39:06.000
that inflammasome complex goes berserk,

00:39:06.000 --> 00:39:08.370
and then one thing leads to another

00:39:08.370 --> 00:39:10.170
and you can't switch it off.

00:39:10.170 --> 00:39:11.940
So, it hasn't been proven
in humans, of course,

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:15.580
but in cells and in mice it has been shown

00:39:15.580 --> 00:39:18.210
that low NAD in those older animals

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:19.983
leads to this hyperactivation.

00:39:21.670 --> 00:39:22.950
- Wow. Wow.

00:39:22.950 --> 00:39:25.810
Well, I could, as you know,

00:39:25.810 --> 00:39:27.790
you probably had trouble
getting away from me

00:39:27.790 --> 00:39:29.710
when I met you a couple of years ago.

00:39:29.710 --> 00:39:31.130
We could talk for hours,

00:39:31.130 --> 00:39:33.570
but we're already well
over the scheduled time.

00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:35.370
But thank you so much, David,

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:37.050
for joining and having this conversation,

00:39:37.050 --> 00:39:39.403
which I, clearly, am deeply intrigued by.

00:39:41.030 --> 00:39:42.390
- Well, Sal, it's great to see you again,

00:39:42.390 --> 00:39:44.530
and thanks for having me on.

00:39:44.530 --> 00:39:45.363
- Yeah.

00:39:45.363 --> 00:39:48.450
Well, thanks everyone for
joining today, as always.

00:39:48.450 --> 00:39:50.627
Fascinating conversation.

00:39:50.627 --> 00:39:52.460
I wasn't joking when I said
I followed David's work

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:54.840
for many decades and have
his book right over there.

00:39:54.840 --> 00:39:56.920
I recommend folks to take a look at it

00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:58.670
if you've been intrigued
by this conversation.

00:39:58.670 --> 00:40:00.690
It's called "Lifespan".

00:40:00.690 --> 00:40:04.350
But I look forward to seeing you in,

00:40:04.350 --> 00:40:06.910
I think, is it day after
tomorrow or tomorrow?

00:40:06.910 --> 00:40:08.010
Let me see. I have it on the...

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:09.010
Oh, there you go, tomorrow.

